{
  "teamId": 133,
  "year": 2020,
  "narrative": "The 2020 Oakland Athletics arrived at a pandemic-shortened season with something to prove, and they wasted little time making their case. After a rocky July that left them 3-4, the A's caught fire in August, reeling off a 19-8 month that transformed them from a fringe contender into one of baseball's best teams. Big swings from Mark Canha, Ramón Laureano, and Stephen Piscotty powered an offense that punched well above its weight, while a stout pitching staff held opponents at bay. The month of August was Oakland baseball at its finest — scrappy, explosive, and relentless.\n\nSeptember brought turbulence. The A's finished 14-12, a respectable record but one punctuated by costly moments — Frankie Montas and Joakim Soria allowing late-game damage, and the offense occasionally betraying itself at the worst times. Yet Laureano kept finding ways to be the heartbeat of the lineup, launching a clutch home run late in the month to remind everyone what Oakland was capable of when it mattered most. The A's limped into October having secured their playoff spot, but the late-season shakiness left questions hanging in the air.\n\nThe Wild Card round offered no comfort — a stumble that sent them into the ALDS against the Houston Astros with momentum working against them. Oakland fought back in the Division Series, with Chad Pinder and Laureano delivering signature moments and the team clawing to keep its season alive. But the Astros, forged in October baseball, proved too much. Carlos Correa's three-run blast off Frankie Montas in Game 4 served as the punctuation mark on a series that slipped away, closing the book on a season that was thrilling, maddening, and ultimately just short of where this group of A's believed they could go.",
